1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,350
The Rust scheduler in doeff: one scheduler, with two implementations.

2
00:00:06,900 --> 00:00:12,000
In doeff, concurrency is effects too. The program yields Spawn and Gather,

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,250
and scheduled is the handler that answers them, resuming k with each answer.

4
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:24,650
That handler comes in Rust and in Python, with the same meaning. Rust is the default; Python is opt-in.

5
00:00:26,300 --> 00:00:33,200
Which one runs? The argument decides first, then the environment variable DOEFF SCHEDULER, then the default.

6
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:41,500
The default is rust. Any other name is refused with a ValueError.

7
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:49,650
Same meaning: here is one program, run under both implementations.

8
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:54,883
Tasks a and b wait on one promise, and task b has high priority.

9
00:00:54,883 --> 00:00:58,400
Task slow waits on a promise that is never kept.

10
00:00:58,950 --> 00:01:06,200
The opener keeps the promise. Task b wakes first, then task a, in both implementations.

11
00:01:06,750 --> 00:01:14,200
Cancel unwinds task slow: its finally block runs, and then Wait raises TaskCancelledError.

12
00:01:14,750 --> 00:01:19,700
The same result, the same order, and the same sixty-four trace events.

13
00:01:22,250 --> 00:01:27,800
What moved to Rust is every decision the scheduler makes for an effect: task state,

14
00:01:27,800 --> 00:01:31,000
the ready queue, Cancel, and deadlock detection.

15
00:01:31,550 --> 00:01:35,800
What stays in Python is every class your code sees, raises,

16
00:01:35,800 --> 00:01:40,850
or subclasses, along with the warnings, the trace, and the step limit.

17
00:01:41,400 --> 00:01:48,400
So under both, a subclass of an effect resolves as its parent, and Spawn still gives back a Python Task.

18
00:01:52,400 --> 00:01:59,550
And it is faster. Here is the page's benchmark, run again: microseconds of CPU time per operation.

19
00:02:01,400 --> 00:02:07,500
Wherever the scheduler does the work, Rust takes six to twelve times less CPU time.

20
00:02:09,900 --> 00:02:16,900
An effect that only passes through gains least, because its time is spent outside the scheduler.

21
00:02:19,400 --> 00:02:26,150
The Rust scheduler: the same effects, the same handler, the same meaning, in a fraction of the time.
